PDP-11 and Pro 300 series Fortran IV and Fortran-77 Year 2000 compliance
========================================================================

DATTST4 is a program to check the Y2K compliance of RT-11 or RSX Fortran-77;
it is written for RSX but can be used for RT-11 if one links it with a dummy
GETTIM routine:

  gettim::
    rts pc
    .end

or if one just comments (or "D"'s) out the gettim call.  DATE4 is not in
F77 V5 but might be in new Y2K compliant versions sold by Mentec.  In any
case, the f77y2k.mac file will patch an old RT-11 Fortran-77 that is not
Y2K compliant, and f77date.mac does so for RSX and P/OS Fortran-77 (no Y2K
update of P/OS Fortran (PROF77) (or other RT-11 and P/OS products for the
Pro 300 series computers) is planned, so the users must step in and do it).

Though written for Fortran-77, there is no reason why it should not work
with Fortran IV as well.  They use the same PDP-11 R5 calling sequence and
the same DATE and IDATE functions.

Actually, the Y2K bug in old Fortran is quite trivial.  In RT-11, the DATE
function, if not patched, will act like unpatched DIR and print the date
in the "colon" format until the end of 2003, then it won't print the month
and year at all.  In RSX, it will use the "colon" format for awhile, then
print gibberish for the year later in the 21st century, though the day and
month will be usable.

This is corrected for both Fortrans ... see the .mac file for details.  In
order to use it, just assemble the appropriate file into an .obj and
then link the appropriate .obj with the program you are compiling, as in
the example dattst4.cmd file.

For RSX, the integer year returned by idate is the same as that returned
by GETTIM (GTIM$ directive).  For RT-11, I saw no need to do that, and it
makes the fix quite trivial to just return a value of 0-72 for the years
2000-2072.  If it is desired to use 100-172 for these years, or extend it
to 2099, this is easily done by editing the source code.  The RSX version
will work for as long as GTIM$ will return a value, which for RSX-11M+ 
V4.5 is through 1-JAN-2900 (1000. years from 1-JAN-1900).  Since the MCR
TIM command will not accept a date if the year is above 2899 A.D., then
this year can only be obtained (for now) by letting the year roll over
from December 31.  There is plenty of time to deal with Y2.9K anyway ;-).

Example of usage on 14-MAR-2000:

	character*9  day
	character*11 day4
	call idate(imonth,iday,iyear)
	print*,imonth,iday,iyear         ! e.g. 3,14,100 (RSX) or 3,14,0 (RT-11)
	call date(day)
	call date4(day4)
	print*,day,day4			 ! e.g. 14-MAR-00 14-MAR-2000 (all OS's)
	call exit
	end

Floating-point information
--------------------------

The GBLPAT statements in the command file may be omitted if running under
RSX11M (not Plus) and/or if it is not desired to ever run the program on a
system without an FPU.  The GBLPAT statements can be applied to any RSX
Fortran-77 program to NOP out three instances of unnecessary
floating-point instructions which are included from the F77 OTS even if no
FPP instructions are generated in the code itself.  The patch enables the
program to run without an FPU (IF there are floating point references in
the source code).  Note that the patch may need to be modified if using a
different version of the Fortran-77 OTS;  the OTS is found in a file such
as [1,1]F77OTS.OLB or [1,5]PROF77.OLB or in a resident library with a .TSK
extension.  Examining the executable program with ODT or another debugger
will show that the initial JSR PC, OTI$ in all Fortran programs calls a
floating-point initialize routine shortly before exiting; this is replaced
by two NOP's along with another NOP for the BCS following it which will
cause an Error 2 if not removed.  The other two patches are for an
initialize and exit routine which store and load the FPU program status
respectively (STFPS 202(R3), LDFPS 202(R3)) and are replaced with 2 NOP's
each.  It is possible that other OTS versions may have these instructions
at different offsets; one can use DMP, ODT or ZAP to look for these
instructions, and they can be manually patched with ZAP as well (the OTI$
call can be easily ZAPped out with a ZAP command file (available on
request), but the other two patches are better done via TKB or PAB as in
the .CMD file, or manually with ZAP (run the program and note the two PC
locations where a reserved instruction trap occurs; examining those
locations with ZAP shows it is where the STFPS and LDFPS are). 

If one does not call the routine to initialize the FPP and the FPP
SST's from OTI$, and one does not use any FPP instructions in one's
program, then of course there is no need to save and restore the FPP
status as the OTS does.  If your program does include FPP statements, then
do not include the GBLPAT statements in the linking process.

GBLPAT cannot be used with FTB, the Fast Task Builder; one must use TKB or
PAB.
